# Git Conventions

## Commit Message Format

```text
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

- Bullet 1 describing what changed and why.
- Bullet 2 with key impacted areas or behaviors.
- Bullet 3 for notable implementation notes if needed.
```

- Use imperative, present tense; no trailing period in subject
- Keep subject ≤ 72 chars

### Types
- `feat` `fix` `docs` `style` `refactor` `perf` `test` `build` `ci` `chore` `revert`

### Scopes
- `www` – Next.js app under `apps/www`
- `api` – Hono API under `apps/api`
- `repo` – Monorepo-level changes (tooling, config)

### Examples

```text
feat(www): replace topbar with resizable navbar

- Implement desktop/mobile navbar using motion-based components.
- Keep existing routes and theme toggle intact.
- Adjust gradient opacity on scroll for subtle contrast.
```

```text
fix(api): validate pagination params and return 400 on invalid input

- Add input parsing in handlers; ensure numeric bounds.
- Return typed JSON error payloads consistently.
```

```text
chore(repo): update ESLint and Tailwind to latest minor

- Align configs across packages.
```

## Branch Naming

```text
<type>/<short-kebab-description>
```

Examples:

```text
feature/resizable-navbar
bugfix/navbar-opacity-on-scroll
chore/update-deps
```

## Lefthook & Commit Safety

- Prefer fixing hook issues with minimal, focused edits.
- Conditional `--no-verify` allowed if failures are non-critical and out-of-scope (e.g., stylistic warnings in untouched files).
- Do not bypass for type/build/test failures in changed areas or commit message validation.
- When bypassing, note the reason briefly in the commit output.
